> "The clarity cost is the hidden tax on asscher. A client shows me a $36,991 asscher and a $44,994 radiant and thinks the asscher is the deal. But that asscher is VVS2. The equivalent grade in radiant — G-VS1 — is $44,994. The radiant in G-VS2 is $17,714 at 2ct. The step facets are costing them money." — Farzana, diamond consultant

## The Clarity Buffer

Step cuts and brilliant cuts handle inclusions completely differently. This is the single most important thing to understand before choosing between asscher and radiant.

In an asscher, the long parallel facets work like mirrors in a corridor. When a feather or crystal sits inside the stone, those facets reflect it — you see it once, then again in the mirror, then again. A VS2 inclusion that would be invisible in a round brilliant can look like a VS1 in an asscher.

This is why every experienced gemologist recommends VS1 minimum for asscher cuts, and many recommend VS2 only after a loupe inspection of that specific stone.

In a radiant, the 70 brilliant-style facets create constant light scatter. An inclusion that sits in the table gets broken up into dozens of tiny light events — the facets compete with the flaw rather than magnifying it. A good SI1 in a radiant is often completely eye-clean.

This is not true for asscher.

This has a direct price impact. If you are buying a 1ct stone:
- Radiant: you can buy [G-VS2 at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and be eye-clean
- Asscher: you need VS1 minimum, which starts at [E-VS1 for $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

At 2ct, that gap widens significantly. You can buy a [2ct radiant D-VS2 at $17,714](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/16750163?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with confidence. A 2ct asscher in VS2 is risky — most gemologists would tell you to go VS1 at minimum, which pushes you to [G-VS2 asscher at $21,147](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27103714?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and higher.

The clarity buffer means radiant is the more forgiving shape for buyers who want to maximize carat size within a fixed budget.

